{
  "dom_id": "step-q72075",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "An oil tank is leaking. The volume of oil in the tank, in liters, after $t$ hours is given by the function $q(t).$ The values of $q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)$ | $200$ | $192$ | $186$ | $182$ | $180$ |\n\nApproximate the rate at which the volume of oil in the tank is changing when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{\u2032}(2) & \u2248\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =186-192 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of change of the volume is $-6$ liters per hour.",
